  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A warming trend peaks Friday, with a cold air \nwedge building in for the weekend behind a cold front passage.\n\n \nUpper level ridging is expected to build over the southeast \nstates, with the northern periphery likely across the Ohio \nvalley and Mid- Atlantic states region. This will allow for \nincreasing temperatures through Friday before the arrival of a \ncold front pushes into the region late on Friday. \n\nBehind this cold front, surface high pressure is expected to \nslide from the northern Great lakes region into the Northeast, \nwhich will briefly lead to cold air wedging developing over \nVA/NC Saturday and Sunday. This will dramatically cool \ntemperatures areawide back to normal or just below normal for \nthe weekend. The possibility of a secondary shortwave trough and\nstronger cold front look to push into the region late Monday \ninto Tuesday, which looks to reinforce the cooler air into\nmidweek.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The current period of dry and clear weather ends \nlate Friday into the weekend with increasing chances for rain. \n\nWith the approach of a cold front on Friday night, and moisture\nfrom the remnants from Polo overriding the front, the region\nlooks to be in store for a couple days of cool and rainy\nconditions through the weekend. Specifics on rainfall totals are\nstill uncertain at this time, as ensemble model guidance has\nshifted towards the surface high pressure and dry air aloft\ninhibiting rainfall production across the area. The cutoff line\nof dry vs moist air is quite evident and sharp, and the\nplacement of this line as the frontal boundary stalls will play\na great role in how much precipitation the area sees over the\nweekend. If this line remains across northern VA, much of the\narea will see beneficial rainfall; however, if this line of\ndrier air penetrates further south beyond the I-64 corridor,\nthen much of the area will miss out on any heavy rainfall.\n\nWe dry out next week.",
